# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: Right Hand Leaves Before the Ferry

**Story & Context:** At 170 BPM, Naree opens with a four-note right-hand khim cell locked close to the snare grid while her left hand answers slightly late with two lower tones. Ellis cuts a human break so the ghost snare follows the right hand for eight bars, then switches Break Shadow to the left. Arun waits for Naree’s first hard damping gesture before entering with a short fretless fifth-to-sixth slide. Mira adds one rootless minor-9 Rhodes chord after the cutoff, not under the attack. The chorus tightens the Twin Clock Offset without eliminating it, making the groove feel more urgent without adding layers.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Rainy ferry-ticket window after closing, one punched ticket trapped under glass, two red time stamps printed a few seconds apart, khim mallets resting across an old fare table. Original sparse lyric fragments: “right hand left first / ferry still tied / two clocks on one ticket / rain erased the minute.”

## Track 2: Stamp Delay at Pier Nine

**Story & Context:** At 174 BPM, this is the EP’s most technical breakbeat track. Ellis uses chopped live drums with very low ghost-note levels and abrupt cymbal gaps. Naree performs alternating two-hand figures where every third left-hand note is damped immediately. Each Damp Cut Relay opens a different response: fretless bass, bass flute, or a dry Rhodes stab. Midway through, a Tremolo Freeze holds one upper khim dyad for four bars while the break remains nearly unchanged beneath it. The expected build never comes. Instead, a Half-Bar Clearway removes keys, bass, and flute just before the groove returns.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Old pier-nine ticket stamp on a scratched metal counter, ink pad half open, rainwater spreading across printed departure times, one khim string reflected in the steel surface. Original sparse lyric fragments: “stamp delay / pier nine / the paper says now / the boat says later.”

## Track 3: Two Clocks Under the Awning

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 164 BPM and begins with khim, Rhodes, and no sub. Naree’s right hand plays slow anchor tones while the left hand moves in shorter displaced cells. Kanya answers only the damped notes with breath-heavy bass-flute thirds. Ellis introduces a restrained break after sixteen bars and deliberately leaves several ghost positions empty as Mallet Vacancies. Arun’s fretless line becomes most active in the final section but never doubles the khim. In the final minute, the break is reduced to kick, one dry snare, and room noise. Naree plays one last paired strike; the right-hand string is damped immediately while the left-hand resonance continues alone. The EP ends before the two clocks realign.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Empty river-transit awning after midnight, two wall clocks showing slightly different times, one khim case on a bench, punched ticket rolls moving in the wet wind. Original sparse lyric fragments: “two clocks under the awning / neither one is wrong / left hand still ringing / last ferry already gone.”

